                <pb facs="1051" n="737" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg737"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 701.6361/4</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé d’Affaires in Russia</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">St. Petersburg</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-08-08T10:00:00+02:30">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 8, 1914, 10 a.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received August 9, 11 a.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Summoned urgently to the Austrian Embassy 2 [o’clock], morning, being informed
                    Russian officers and soldiers had entered. Upon arrival found them preparing to
                    remove from the Embassy garage the automobiles belonging to members of Embassy.
                    Protested on ground that nobody could enter premises without my consent,
                    building being under protection of the Government of the United States. Reply
                    given that they must obey orders Ministry War. I called Foreign Office by
                    telephone and again protested. Finally Ministry War gave orders to withdraw
                    without motors. Foreign Office states legal department will decide as to rights
                    of Russian Government to take motors, but I feel unauthorized entrance of
                    Embassy premises by Russian officers is grave matter, especially coming after
                    destruction of German Embassy through negligence of Russian Government. Please
                    wire instructions; also whether I shall surrender automobiles if requested
                    through Foreign Office.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
